{
  "dom_id": "step-q90826",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)={x}^{2}+1?$\n\nI. The range of $f$ is $(1,\\infty )$\n\nII. $∀\\,y\\in [1,\\infty ),$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n\nIII. $f$ maps $R$ **into** $R$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14274151/assets/aac36c0d6798cb74002cf5f68d2b19b16371558c392f7b4080bab03b3fee35f4.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. From the graph, the lowest $y$-value is $1,$ and it occurs at the point $(0,1).$ The graph will cover all $y$-values higher than $y=1$, including $y=1$ itself. So the range of the function is $[1,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of the function is $[1,\\infty ),$ then for all $y$ contained within $[1,\\infty ),$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x$ such that $f(x)=0\\in R.$ So, $f$ maps $R$ *into* $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
